with v(task_key,title,body,why_it_matters,minutes) as (values ('RTN001','Say the good thing','Notice one thing they handled today that you would normally leave unspoken. Tell them you noticed.','Small acknowledgements interrupt the tendency to filter familiar effort into the background.',1),
('RTN002','Catch the autopilot','Pick one routine moment you usually do on autopilot. Watch what each of you does, and name the pattern privately to yourself.','Noticing a cue-response pattern is the first step toward choosing a different response.',1),
('RTN003','One real follow-up','Ask one genuine follow-up question about something your partner tells you today before sharing your own story.','A small change in attention can make an ordinary exchange feel less automatic and more responsive.',2),
('RTN004','Look up','Give them your full attention for ten seconds when you first see each other today. Put down what you''re doing, look up, and really greet them.','Deliberately shifting attention can interrupt familiar moments that have become almost invisible.',1),
('RTN005','Catch the story','Catch one annoyed thought about your partner today. Name the story your mind is telling you before you react.','Automatic interpretations can shape a reaction before we realize an interpretation has happened.',1),
('RTN006','Find one detail','Notice one detail about your partner you haven''t paid attention to lately—their clothes, expression, music, food choice, or something they''re absorbed in.','Familiarity can narrow attention. Deliberately noticing detail makes the familiar visible again.',1),
('RTN007','Ask beyond logistics','Ask your partner one question today that has nothing to do with schedules, chores, money, food, or plans.','Moving one conversation beyond coordination creates a small opening for curiosity.',2),
('RTN008','Catch the bid','Catch one small attempt your partner makes to connect—a joke, photo, comment, question, or look—and give it a real response.','Responding to small bids for attention can change the feel of everyday interaction without requiring a big conversation.',2),
('RTN009','Remember what drew you in','Bring to mind one thing that first drew you to your partner. Tell them, or simply carry it with you the next time you see them.','Bringing an older positive representation to mind can interrupt seeing a familiar person only through today''s logistics.',1),
('RTN010','Five minutes, no admin','Spend five minutes together without talking about chores, schedules, work admin, or tomorrow''s plans.','Briefly removing logistical talk creates room for a different kind of interaction.',5),
('RTN011','Take a new route','Take a walk together and choose streets you''ve never walked down before. No destination needed—just explore for ten minutes.','Tiny environmental changes can interrupt autopilot and create fresh shared attention.',5),
('RTN012','Choose for each other','Get a coffee, snack, or small treat together and choose for each other. Keep it within things you already know the other person likes.','A low-stakes change to a familiar choice introduces novelty and attention without requiring a special date.',5),
('RTN013','Send the unexpected thing','Send them something during the day that isn''t practical: a photo, thought, joke, or tiny thing that made you think of them.','Changing a communication channel from coordination to connection adds novelty to an ordinary day.',2),
('RTN014','Hand over one choice','Give your partner one choice you usually make today—the music, route, show, dinner, or where you sit—and go with their pick.','Changing a predictable micro-routine creates a safe behavioral experiment in flexibility.',2),
('RTN015','Tiny surprise','Do one small useful or enjoyable thing they aren''t expecting today. Keep it small enough that it doesn''t create an obligation to react.','A low-pressure positive surprise can interrupt predictability while reinforcing attention to the other person.',5),
('RTN016','Try their pick','Choose one low-stakes thing your partner likes that you usually wouldn''t pick—song, snack, short video, route, or activity—and join them in it once.','Trying the other person''s preference is a small approach behavior that breaks personal defaults.',10),
('RTN017','Add ten minutes','Turn one errand together into ten extra minutes. Walk another block, sit somewhere, or wander into a place you''ve never stopped at.','Adding a small rewarding element to an existing routine is easier than creating a separate event from scratch.',10),
('RTN018','Sit somewhere new','Choose a different table, bench, corner, or side of the room in a place you both know well.','A tiny environmental change can make a familiar setting more noticeable again.',1),
('RTN019','Bring back a song','Play one song connected to a good shared memory while you''re together or send it to them without a long explanation.','A familiar positive cue can reactivate shared memory while changing the emotional texture of an ordinary moment.',4),
('RTN020','Let the phone wait','Choose one ordinary five-minute moment together today and leave your phone out of reach until the moment is over.','Changing the environment removes a competing cue and makes attention easier rather than relying on willpower.',5),
('RTN021','Invite them along','Invite your partner into one thing you''d normally do alone today—a walk, shop run, coffee, cooking, or quick errand.','An invitation turns an existing routine into an opportunity for shared activity without adding much effort.',10),
('RTN022','Find one new corner','Spend five minutes together somewhere you''ve never stopped before—a side street, bench, little shop, courtyard, or view.','Small doses of novelty can make shared time feel less interchangeable.',5),
('RTN023','Reverse one role','For one small routine today, swap who normally does it or who takes the lead.','Changing a familiar role can expose hidden assumptions and make an automatic division of labor visible.',10),
('RTN024','Ask for a micro-adventure','Offer two tiny options that are different from the usual—walk after dinner or dessert outside, new café or old favorite—and let them pick.','Constrained choices make novelty easier to act on than vague plans to ''do more together.''',15),
('RTN025','Change the first minute','Pick one recurring moment today—waking up, coming home, dinner, getting into the car—and deliberately change only its first minute.','Changing the start of a familiar sequence can disrupt the rest of an automatic pattern with very little effort.',1),
('RTN026','Add one playful choice','Turn one boring decision today into a playful one: coin flip the takeaway, each pick a mystery snack, or choose the next song for each other.','Play introduces positive affect into routines that normally run on efficiency alone.',3),
('RTN027','Pause one beat','Take one breath before answering the first time you feel a sharp, dismissive, or impatient reply coming.','A short pause creates room between cue and response, making an alternative behavior possible.',1),
('RTN028','What else could it mean?','Catch one moment when you decide what your partner meant. Ask yourself, “What else could this mean?” before responding.','Generating an alternative interpretation can loosen the link between an assumption and an automatic reaction.',1),
('RTN029','Finish hearing it','Let your partner fully finish one story or point today before you start preparing your answer.','Shifting attention from reply-planning to listening changes a common automatic conversational pattern.',3),
('RTN030','Answer the feeling first','Respond to the feeling first when your partner tells you about something frustrating today. Leave advice and fixing for later.','Changing from automatic problem-solving to responsiveness can make the other person feel heard without requiring a long discussion.',2),
('RTN031','Say it softer','Say one difficult point in a calmer first sentence today. Keep the point; change the delivery.','Changing delivery while keeping the underlying need intact tests whether the interaction pattern can shift without avoidance.',2),
('RTN032','Repair the small miss','Repair one small miss as soon as you notice it. Say, “That came out wrong,” or “Sorry, I wasn''t listening,” and try the moment again.','Quick repair prevents a minor automatic response from becoming the tone of the interaction.',1),
('RTN033','Make one clean request','Turn one “Why do you always...?” thought into a concrete request about what you need right now.','A specific request reduces mind-reading and global judgments while preserving the underlying need.',2),
('RTN034','Drop the scoreboard once','Catch yourself mentally counting who did more once today. Decide what you need, then ask for it plainly or let this one go.','Moving from internal scorekeeping to a specific choice interrupts resentment-building without denying genuine imbalance.',2),
('RTN035','Try one generous reading','Choose one ambiguous thing your partner does today and give it the most reasonable non-hostile explanation that fits the facts.','Testing a less threatening interpretation can reduce automatic escalation when intent is genuinely unclear.',1),
('RTN036','Turn toward','Give your partner your full attention for ten seconds the next time they show you something or make a small comment. Then return to what you were doing.','A brief deliberate response replaces automatic partial attention with a clear moment of responsiveness.',1),
('RTN037','Ask before fixing','Ask, “Do you want ideas or do you want me to listen?” the first time your partner brings you a problem today.','A simple choice interrupts habitual fixing and makes support more responsive to what is actually wanted.',1),
('RTN038','Reset outside','Take a ten-minute walk together when the mood at home feels stuck. Leave the problem indoors for the first five minutes.','Changing context can interrupt environmental cues that help maintain an unproductive interaction pattern.',10),
('RTN039','One different goodbye','Before one separation today—work, errands, sleep, travel—make the goodbye slightly more intentional than usual: eye contact, a kind sentence, or a proper ''see you later.''','Recurring transition moments are strong cues for small repeatable changes.',1),
('RTN040','Rewrite the first reply','Rewrite one irritated text before sending it. Remove the sentence you wouldn''t say calmly face-to-face.','A small delay can interrupt rapid interpretation-response loops that are amplified in text.',2),
('RTN041','Pick one keeper','Choose one Move from the last three weeks that genuinely improved a moment between you. Mark that one as worth repeating.','Selecting a behavior that produced a useful result makes repetition intentional rather than relying on memory.',2),
('RTN042','Attach it to a cue','Take one Keeper and decide exactly when it will happen again: after coffee, when you get home, on the walk to the shop, or before sleep.','Linking a behavior to an existing recurring cue makes remembering it easier.',2),
('RTN043','Make it smaller','Take a Move you liked but found hard to repeat. Reduce it until it feels almost too easy to skip: twenty minutes becomes five; a big plan becomes one question.','Reducing friction makes repetition more likely and helps separate the useful behavior from unnecessary effort.',3),
('RTN044','Repeat the greeting','Choose one arrival or reunion today and repeat the intentional greeting you tried earlier: stop, look up, and properly acknowledge each other.','Repeating a behavior at the same cue strengthens the cue-behavior link.',1),
('RTN045','Protect five minutes','Choose one five-minute window that worked well for you—coffee, after dinner, a walk, before bed—and make it deliberately phone-free again today.','Repeating a useful environmental setup can turn attention into a routine rather than a daily decision.',5),
('RTN046','Keep the outside ritual','Pick one tiny thing outside the house you could realistically repeat weekly: a walk, coffee stop, market lap, bench, or errand detour. Do it or choose when you''ll do it next.','Attaching connection to an existing place or routine lowers the effort needed to repeat it.',10),
('RTN047','Keep the question','Choose one question that opened a better conversation this month. Ask it again in a new context rather than searching for a brand-new technique.','Repeating a successful conversational behavior helps turn it into a natural response.',3),
('RTN048','Plan for the bad day','Choose one Keeper and finish this sentence privately: ''Even on a busy or bad day, I can still do the two-minute version by…''','Planning a minimum version protects a new behavior when motivation and time are low.',2),
('RTN049','Make the cue visible','For one Keeper, change something small in the environment so it reminds you: put the phones away at dinner, leave walking shoes by the door, or add a private calendar cue.','Environmental prompts reduce the need to remember a new behavior at exactly the right moment.',3),
('RTN050','Name what changed','Tonight, name one interaction that feels different from four weeks ago and the small behavior that helped change it.','Connecting a useful outcome to a specific behavior reinforces what is worth carrying forward.',3))
update private.task_catalogue c set title=v.title,body=v.body,why_it_matters=v.why_it_matters,minutes=v.minutes
from v where c.task_key=v.task_key and c.content_version=2;